<title>FRANCE: Breakdown of OECD Economic Outlook forecasts.</title>
<headline>Breakdown of OECD Economic Outlook forecasts.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1996-12-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Following are tables giving key economic indicator forecasts for the Group of Seven (G7) leading industrialised nations given in the semi-annual report released by the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) on Thursday:</p>
<p>REAL GDP (Percentage changes from previous period):</p>
<p>				  Estimates and projections</p>
<p>			    1996	     1997	     1998</p>
<p> United States	   2.4		2.2		2.0</p>
<p> Japan		     3.6		1.6		3.7</p>
<p> Germany		   1.1		2.2		2.6</p>
<p> France		    1.3		2.5		2.6</p>
<p> Italy		     0.8		1.2		2.1</p>
<p> United Kingdom	  2.4		3.3		3.0</p>
<p> Canada		    1.5		3.3		3.3</p>
<p> Total G7		  2.2		2.1		2.6</p>
<p> Total OECD		2.4		2.4		2.7</p>
<p> European Union	  1.6		2.4		2.7</p>
<p>REAL GDP (Percentage changes from previous period):</p>
<p>			    1996	     1997	     1998</p>
<p> (Half years)	 1	2	 1	2	 1	2</p>
<p> United States     2.2    3.0     1.9    1.9     2.1    2.1</p>
<p> Japan		 6.4   -1.2     2.2    3.2     3.9    3.9</p>
<p> Germany	     1.0    1.8     2.3    2.5     2.6    2.7</p>
<p> France		1.5    2.2     2.5    2.6     2.6    2.6</p>
<p> Italy		 0.5    0.8     1.1    1.7     2.1    2.4</p>
<p> United Kingdom    2.1    3.5     3.1    3.3     3.0    2.8</p>
<p> Canada		1.2    3.2     3.2    3.3     3.3    3.2</p>
<p> European Union    1.6    2.1     2.4    2.6     2.7    2.7</p>
<p> Total OECD	  2.9    2.2     2.4    2.6     2.8    2.8</p>
<p>REAL TOTAL DOMESTIC DEMAND</p>
<p>	   (Percentage changes from previous period):</p>
<p> United States     2.7    3.4     1.5    1.8     1.9    2.0</p>
<p> Japan		 7.9   -1.4     2.4    3.1     3.7    3.6</p>
<p> Germany	     0.5    1.3     1.7    2.2     2.5    2.6</p>
<p> France		0.3    2.0     2.3    2.5     2.6    2.5</p>
<p> Italy		-0.7    0.3     0.9    1.7     2.2    2.4</p>
<p> United Kingdom    2.5    3.4     3.7    3.4     3.1    3.0</p>
<p> Canada		0.9    2.6     3.0    3.3     3.5    3.4</p>
<p> European Union    1.0    1.9     2.2    2.5     2.7    2.8</p>
<p> Total OECD	  3.1    2.4     2.2    2.5     2.8    2.8</p>
<p>INFLATION</p>
<p>(GDP deflator, percentage changes from previous period):</p>
<p> United States     2.0    1.8     2.3    2.1     2.1    2.1</p>
<p> Japan		 0.2   -0.6     0.9    0.7    -0.3   -0.1</p>
<p> Germany	     1.1    1.1     1.2    1.3     1.3    1.3</p>
<p> France		1.8    1.4     1.2    1.3     1.4    1.4</p>
<p> Italy		 3.4    3.1     2.7    2.5     2.5    2.5</p>
<p> United Kingdom    2.4    2.6     2.2    1.9     1.9    2.0</p>
<p> Canada		0.3    1.6     1.4    1.4     1.5    1.5</p>
<p>Total OECD (less high inflation countries)</p>
<p>	 *	     1.7    1.5     1.9    1.8     1.6    1.7</p>
<p> European Union    2.3    2.2     2.0    2.0     2.0    2.0</p>
<p> Total OECD	  4.2    3.6     3.7    3.5     3.2    2.9</p>
<p>* High inflation countries are defined as countries which have had 10 per cent or more inflation in tems of the GDP deflator on  average during the 1990s on the basis of historical data. Consequently, the Czech Republic, Greece, Hungary, Mexico, Poland and Turkey are excluded from the aggregate.</p>
<p>UNEMPLOYMENT</p>
<p>(per cent of labour force):</p>
<p> United States     5.5    5.2     5.3    5.4     5.5    5.5</p>
